## The question you're answering

A small pop-up café operates from a converted shipping container at
outdoor weekend events. Staff need to take card payments on tablet
devices from anywhere within a 15-metre radius of the container.
There is no fixed broadband line at the sites the café visits.
Recommend ONE networking solution suitable for this scenario and
explain why it suits. (4 marks)

The previous two worked examples taught you to write Impact (M.1.1)
and to write Name (M.2.1). This one teaches you how Explain earns its
mark.

Read the question again. "Recommend ONE networking solution suitable
for this scenario and explain why it suits." The "and explain why it
suits" is doing the work. The mark scheme is not awarding a definition
of whatever you name. It is awarding an explanation of why your choice
fits *this specific café, with these specific constraints*. The
container. The 15-metre radius. The absence of fixed broadband. The
tablets. The weekend events.

A student who explains the concept perfectly but never applies it to
the scenario loses the Explain mark even though the explanation is
correct. This is the pattern marker reports name most often: students
who clearly know what a thing is but cannot show how it suits the
situation.

As in M.1.1, the answers below are highlighted to show structure
inline. The {% role name="name" %}Name{% /role %} is tinted green, the
{% role name="explain" %}Explain{% /role %} is tinted gold, and the
{% role name="impact" %}Impact{% /role %} is tinted orange. A missing
colour means the corresponding component is absent or too weak to
earn its mark.

## Answer One: the textbook definition

> {% role name="name" %}Wi-Fi would be a good solution.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}Wi-Fi is a wireless
> network technology that uses radio waves to connect devices to a
> network. Wi-Fi networks are commonly used in homes, offices, and
> public spaces, and most modern laptops, tablets, and phones can
> connect to them automatically.{% /role %} Wi-Fi is convenient because it
> doesn't require cables.

Let me tell you what a marker sees when this reads as the answer to a
specific-scenario question.

**Name.** "Wi-Fi." A wireless networking concept, named. Mark earned,
though the marker will note that Wi-Fi alone does not address the
no-fixed-broadband constraint. The café needs something that
*generates* internet at the site, not just something that rebroadcasts
an existing connection.

**Explain.** This is where the answer loses three marks. The
explanation is correct as a definition: Wi-Fi does use radio waves, it
is found in homes and offices, devices do connect automatically. But
not a single sentence engages with the café. Where is the 15-metre
radius? Where is the no-fixed-broadband constraint? Where is the
weekend-event context? The answer could be pasted verbatim into any
question about Wi-Fi, anywhere, regardless of scenario, and read the
same. That's the diagnostic. If you can lift your Explain paragraph
out of the answer and drop it into a different question without
changing a word, it is a definition, not an explanation.

**Impact.** "Wi-Fi is convenient because it doesn't require cables."
Generic. The café isn't mentioned. The convenience of cable-free
networking applies to every Wi-Fi setup ever. No scenario-specific
consequence is named.

Marks this would earn: 1 out of 4.

## Answer Two: the partial scenario-tie

> {% role name="name" %}A 4G mobile router would suit.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}A 4G mobile router uses
> a SIM card to connect to a mobile network and creates a Wi-Fi
> hotspot that nearby devices can join. The café's tablets can then
> connect to it.{% /role %}

Better than Answer One. Let's see how much better.

**Name.** "4G mobile router." Clean and complete. Unlike Wi-Fi alone,
this names a solution that actually generates internet on site
without a fixed broadband line. Mark earned cleanly.

**Explain.** Closer to a real explanation, but still partial. The
mechanism is correct (SIM card to mobile network to Wi-Fi hotspot to
devices), and the student has tied it to one scenario detail ("the
café's tablets"). But four other scenario details sit in the question
stem and never appear in the answer: the 15-metre radius, the
container setup, the no-fixed-broadband constraint, the weekend-event
context. Single-token scenario tie-back is closer to an explanation
than Answer One's definition, but it is not a full one. The student
knows they should mention the scenario; they have not yet learned to
engage with every relevant specific.

**Impact.** Missing. The answer ends on "tablets can connect", which
is a mechanism continuation, not a consequence. What can the café now
do that it could not otherwise? Not stated.

Marks this would earn: 2 out of 4. One for Name, one for partial
Explain, zero for Impact.

## Answer Three: the full four marks

> {% role name="name" %}A 4G mobile router would suit this café.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}The router
> connects to a mobile network using a SIM card and rebroadcasts the
> connection as a Wi-Fi hotspot the tablets can join, all within an
> enclosure small enough to fit inside the shipping container and a
> coverage range that comfortably exceeds the 15-metre radius staff
> need to move within.{% /role %} {% role name="impact" %}This means the café can take card payments at
> any weekend event without depending on a fixed broadband line at
> each site, keeping the business operational every weekend without
> setup overhead or coverage gaps that would otherwise lose sales.{% /role %}

Walk through it.

**Name.** "4G mobile router." Clean. Same as Answer Two.

**Explain.** This is where Answer Three earns the mark Answer Two
missed. The mechanism (SIM card to mobile network to Wi-Fi hotspot to
tablets) is still there, but now every relevant scenario detail is
engaged with: the container ("small enough to fit inside the shipping
container"), the 15-metre radius ("coverage range that comfortably
exceeds"), the tablets ("the tablets can join"), and by implication
the no-fixed-broadband constraint (the router generates its own
connection via SIM). The explanation is not a definition; it is a fit
argument. Each scenario detail is touched and tied back to a property
of the named solution.

**Impact.** "The café can take card payments at any weekend event
without depending on a fixed broadband line, keeping the business
operational every weekend without setup overhead or coverage gaps
that would otherwise lose sales." Concrete business consequence,
scenario-tied (weekend events, no broadband, mobility). And it goes
further: it names the cost of *not* having the router (lost sales).
This is the kind of Impact that distinguishes a 3-mark answer from a
4-mark one.

Marks this would earn: 4 out of 4.

## What to take from all three

The lesson here is the one your future marker will most often write
in red ink in the margin of your script: "explain why it suits *this*
scenario."

Whenever a question contains the word "explain" or "discuss" plus a
specific business context, the Explain mark is for application, not
for definition. The diagnostic from Answer One holds across every
extended-answer question on either paper. If your Explain paragraph
could be lifted out of the answer and dropped into a different
question without changing a word, it is not an explanation, it is a
definition.

Before you commit your answer, run this test. Read your Explain
sentence. Ask yourself: which detail from the scenario is this
sentence engaging with? If the answer is "none," rewrite. If the
answer is "one," look at the scenario again. There are usually three
or four details, and a strong Explain engages with most of them.
